// prism_node -- the backend-agnostic node-tree value type at the foundation of NOVA Prism.
// Every widget (widget/, milestone MA.3), every backend (HTML render at render/, ANSI at
// backend/ansi/, the eventual DOM/GPU backends), and every dev tool (catalog, inspector,
// RTTI-driven form derivation) builds on THIS type and nothing else. Nothing in this file
// may know about markup, ANSI escapes, or a GPU surface -- see PRISM_SPEC.md Part III
// (the 26-primitive vocabulary) and §16 (resource bounds).
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: enum tag + struct tree, not an all-enum ADT ──────────────────────────
// The obvious ADT design -- one enum variant per widget kind, each carrying its own payload
// shape (`Stack(children: list)`, `Label(text: string)`, `Range(min: float, max: float)`, ...)
// -- was rejected for THIS file. Two reasons:
//   1. This milestone (MA.2) is explicitly the generic node-tree type that MA.3's 26 widget
//      constructors build on top of; if the widget-specific payload shapes were baked into
//      an enum here, adding widget #27 would require editing this foundational file instead
//      of adding a new function in widget/. A single struct with a generic `attrs: dict` is
//      the layer that lets MA.3 stay purely additive.
//   2. A uniform recursive tree (one struct shape, `children: list` of the same struct) is
//      what every backend needs to walk generically -- an all-enum ADT would force every
//      tree-walking function (RTTI, resource-bound counting, the eventual HTML/ANSI
//      renderers) to pattern-match all 22 variants just to reach the common `children` list.
// The ADT is not abandoned, though -- it is applied where it earns its keep: `PrismNodeKind`
// below IS an enum, so the vocabulary stays closed (spec axiom A7) and `prism_node_kind_name`
// is an EXHAUSTIVE match with no wildcard arm. Add a 23rd primitive to the enum without
// updating that match, and the compiler rejects the build -- the concrete payoff of choosing
// an ADT for the tag even though the tree itself is a struct.
//
// ── SELF-REFERENCE: children is a bare `list`, not `list<PrismNode>` ──────────────────────
// `list<PrismNode>` (self-referential generic instantiation) has no verified precedent
// anywhere in this codebase, and NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md's own generics section flags
// generics as "thin/edge-tested ... verify before depending on it deeply." The PROVEN idiom
// for a self-referential tree, used by the compiler's own AST (`Expr.children: list` in
// nova_compiler.nova) and by std/core/tree.nova (`Tree.children: list`), is an untyped
// `list` holding elements of the struct's own type. Verified directly for this file (see the
// design probe in this milestone's report): iterating `for c in children` and reading
// `c.depth` / `c.kind` etc. on the loop variable resolves correctly to PrismNode's fields --
// NOVA's `any`-from-untyped-list values narrow to the concrete struct at first typed use,
// exactly as NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md promises for `any`.
//
// ── RESOURCE BOUNDS (PRISM_SPEC.md §16) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// "Face tree depth: 256" and "Nodes per frame: 100,000", both "Enforced at tree construction."
// Every node caches its own subtree `depth` and `count` AT CONSTRUCTION, computed from its
// (already-validated) children in O(children) time. Without this cache, checking a growing
// tree's depth/count by re-walking the whole subtree on every node added would make building
// an n-node tree O(n^2); with it, every construction is O(1) plus the number of direct
// children. Exceeding a bound returns `Err(string)`, never a crash or unbounded allocation --
// exactly what §16 mandates ("a typed error... never a crash").
//
// ── KEYED IDENTITY (PRISM_SPEC.md §12.6) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `key: string?` is the runtime identity NOVA's static reactivity inference cannot supply for
// itself: static analysis can prove a lot about `items[i]`, but not which physical row a
// runtime `i` denotes across a re-render. `prism_node_keyed` is how MA.3's `each`/`grid`
// primitives (which spec §10 makes `by <selector>` MANDATORY on) attach that identity.
//
// ── CROSS-MODULE CONSTRUCTION (a verified compiler limitation, not a style choice) ─────────
// A user-defined enum's variant constructors (`Stack()`, `Label()`, ...) are ONLY resolvable
// in the same file that declares the enum -- verified directly with a two-file probe: from
// an importing file, both `mod.Variant()` (qualified: "has no exported function 'Variant'")
// and bare `Variant()` after `import mod` (unqualified: "unknown identifier") are rejected by
// the compiler. Only a value of the enum type ALREADY IN HAND may be pattern-matched with
// unqualified variant patterns cross-module (type-directed pattern lookup, a different
// resolution path than constructor-call-position identifier lookup). Concretely, this means
// widget/ (MA.3) and every other consumer of PrismNodeKind CANNOT write `Stack()` themselves
// -- they need a function that returns the value. Hence `prism_kind_*()` below: one
// zero-argument wrapper per variant, all defined here (the only file where the bare
// constructors resolve), so every other file gets kind values through an ordinary
// cross-module function call. Prefixed `prism_kind_*` for the usual flat-symbol-space reason.
// CORRECTION -- the limitation is NOT enum-specific (an earlier version of this note implied
// it was): a plain `type`'s positional constructor call is resolved through the exact same
// constructor-call-position identifier lookup, and is THEREFORE EQUALLY file-local cross-module
// -- confirmed directly in MA.3 part B (`mod.PrismPickOption(...)` / `mod.PrismTabPanel(...)`
// both fail E1000 "has no exported function", the identical failure mode as an enum variant).
// The true scope of this limitation is: constructor-call-position identifier lookup (whether
// the identifier names an enum variant or a struct) is file-local; only type-directed pattern
// matching on an already-typed value works across module boundaries. Every struct with a
// cross-module positional constructor in this codebase (prism_interact.nova's
// `prism_pick_option`, prism_structure.nova's `prism_tab_panel`) needs the same
// ordinary-function-wrapper treatment `prism_kind_*` gets here, for the same underlying reason.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   Vocabulary
//     PrismNodeKind                                       the closed, 22-primitive tag enum
//     PrismNode                                            the node-tree value
//     prism_kind_stack() / prism_kind_band() / ... (22)  -> PrismNodeKind   cross-module ctors
//     prism_node_kind_name(kind)                    -> string   exhaustive kind -> name
//   Constructors (all bounds-checked, never panic on a hostile tree)
//     prism_node_new(kind, attrs, children, key)    -> Result<PrismNode>   the one real ctor
//     prism_node_leaf(kind, attrs)                   -> Result<PrismNode>   no children, no key
//     prism_node_branch(kind, attrs, children)       -> Result<PrismNode>   children, no key
//     prism_node_keyed(kind, attrs, children, key)  -> Result<PrismNode>   children + identity
//   Accessors
//     prism_node_kind(n)               -> PrismNodeKind
//     prism_node_kind_str(n)           -> string
//     prism_node_children(n)           -> list          (of PrismNode)
//     prism_node_child_count(n)        -> int
//     prism_node_key(n)                -> string?
//     prism_node_has_key(n)            -> bool
//     prism_node_depth(n)              -> int           (this node's subtree depth, leaf = 1)
//     prism_node_count(n)              -> int           (this node + every descendant)
//     prism_node_walk(n, visit)                          pre-order visit(node) over the subtree
//   Attributes (typed where possible; attrs itself stays a generic dict -- see design note)
//     prism_node_has_attr(n, name)               -> bool
//     prism_node_attr_str(n, name, default_val)  -> string
//     prism_node_attr_int(n, name, default_val)  -> int
//     prism_node_attr_bool(n, name, default_val) -> bool
//
// RTTI: PrismNode is a plain `type`, so `type_of`/`type_name`/`field_names`/`field_get`/
// `fields` all work on it for free (NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md "Struct reflection API") --
// nothing in this file implements them; the KAT (_kat_prism_node.nova) proves they work.

// ── The closed, owned vocabulary (PRISM_SPEC.md §7-10, Part III) ─────────────────────────
// 22 primitives across four families, exactly as PRISM_SPEC.md Part III enumerates them.
// (PRISM_STATUS.md / PRISM_ROADMAP.md say "26 primitives" in several places; the spec's own
// table in Part III sums to 22. That is a genuine discrepancy between the docs -- flagged
// here, not silently "corrected" to 26 by inventing 4 extra tags with no spec definition.)
enum PrismNodeKind
    // Arrangement (§7)
    Stack()
    Band()
    Layer()
    Mesh()
    Flow()
    Pane()
    Gap()
    // Content (§8)
    Label()
    Art()
    Glyph()
    Draw()
    // Interaction (§9)
    Press()
    Entry()
    Pick()
    Flag()
    Range()
    Link()
    // Structure (§10)
    Each()
    Grid()
    Sheet()
    Hint()
    Tabs()

// ── Cross-module constructors ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// See the header note above: these exist ONLY because a user-defined enum's variant
// constructors do not resolve from outside the file that declares the enum. Every consumer
// of PrismNodeKind in another file (widget/, kat/, and eventually every backend) goes through
// these instead of writing `Stack()` etc. directly, which would not compile there.
fn prism_kind_stack() -> PrismNodeKind
    Stack()
fn prism_kind_band() -> PrismNodeKind
    Band()
fn prism_kind_layer() -> PrismNodeKind
    Layer()
fn prism_kind_mesh() -> PrismNodeKind
    Mesh()
fn prism_kind_flow() -> PrismNodeKind
    Flow()
fn prism_kind_pane() -> PrismNodeKind
    Pane()
fn prism_kind_gap() -> PrismNodeKind
    Gap()
fn prism_kind_label() -> PrismNodeKind
    Label()
fn prism_kind_art() -> PrismNodeKind
    Art()
fn prism_kind_glyph() -> PrismNodeKind
    Glyph()
fn prism_kind_draw() -> PrismNodeKind
    Draw()
fn prism_kind_press() -> PrismNodeKind
    Press()
fn prism_kind_entry() -> PrismNodeKind
    Entry()
fn prism_kind_pick() -> PrismNodeKind
    Pick()
fn prism_kind_flag() -> PrismNodeKind
    Flag()
fn prism_kind_range() -> PrismNodeKind
    Range()
fn prism_kind_link() -> PrismNodeKind
    Link()
fn prism_kind_each() -> PrismNodeKind
    Each()
fn prism_kind_grid() -> PrismNodeKind
    Grid()
fn prism_kind_sheet() -> PrismNodeKind
    Sheet()
fn prism_kind_hint() -> PrismNodeKind
    Hint()
fn prism_kind_tabs() -> PrismNodeKind
    Tabs()

// Human-readable kind name. EXHAUSTIVE match, no `_` wildcard arm: adding a 23rd variant to
// PrismNodeKind without adding its case here is a COMPILE ERROR, not a silent "unknown"
// string returned at runtime. This is the concrete payoff of the enum-tag design (see header).
fn prism_node_kind_name(kind: PrismNodeKind) -> string
    match kind
        Stack() => "stack"
        Band() => "band"
        Layer() => "layer"
        Mesh() => "mesh"
        Flow() => "flow"
        Pane() => "pane"
        Gap() => "gap"
        Label() => "label"
        Art() => "art"
        Glyph() => "glyph"
        Draw() => "draw"
        Press() => "press"
        Entry() => "entry"
        Pick() => "pick"
        Flag() => "flag"
        Range() => "range"
        Link() => "link"
        Each() => "each"
        Grid() => "grid"
        Sheet() => "sheet"
        Hint() => "hint"
        Tabs() => "tabs"

// Every kind value, for consumers that must enumerate the whole vocabulary (the MA.7 catalog, the
// eventual inspector and docs generator). A FUNCTION returning a fresh literal, not a module-level
// `let` -- a module-level `let` holding a list is silently EMPTY when the module is imported
// (verified compiler bug, 2026-08-15; see prism/style/prism_palette.nova's header for the probe).
//
// HONEST LIMITATION, stated rather than hidden: this list can drift from the enum. Adding variant
// #23 makes `prism_node_kind_name` above fail to compile (E1009, because that match is in THIS
// file, where exhaustiveness IS checked -- see spec §17A.3b), which forces the author here; but
// nothing forces them to also extend this list. The KAT therefore asserts len == 22 and that every
// name is distinct and non-empty, which converts a silent omission into a loud test failure. That
// is the strongest check available without compiler support for enumerating an enum's variants.
fn prism_node_kind_all() -> list
    [Stack(), Band(), Layer(), Mesh(), Flow(), Pane(), Gap(), Label(), Art(), Glyph(), Draw(), Press(), Entry(), Pick(), Flag(), Range(), Link(), Each(), Grid(), Sheet(), Hint(), Tabs()]

// ── DERIVED a11y ROLE (PRISM_SPEC.md §9) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §9's role column "is not documentation -- it is the compiler's a11y output... the role can
// never drift from the behaviour, because both come from the same type." That guarantee only
// holds if there is exactly ONE derivation, so it lives HERE, beside the kind it derives from,
// rather than being re-implemented by dev/prism_test (query-by-role), a11y/ (auditing) and the
// backends independently -- three copies would be three chances to disagree, and the drift would
// be invisible until an assistive technology got it wrong.
//
// The six INTERACTION primitives are normative in §9. `pick` and `flag` are listed there with two
// roles each ("combobox / radiogroup", "checkbox / switch"); the primary is returned and the
// variant is an attribute-level refinement for the backend, which is the only layer that knows
// which form it rendered.
// The structural roles below follow the HTML mapping in §17A rather than §9, which does not
// assign them. Kinds with no meaningful role return "" -- deliberately not "generic", so a caller
// can distinguish "no role" from "a role called generic" without a magic string.
fn prism_node_role_of(kind: PrismNodeKind) -> string
    match kind
        Press() => "button"
        Entry() => "textbox"
        Pick() => "combobox"
        Flag() => "checkbox"
        Range() => "slider"
        Link() => "link"
        Grid() => "table"
        Tabs() => "tablist"
        Sheet() => "dialog"
        Hint() => "tooltip"
        Each() => "list"
        Art() => "img"
        Stack() => ""
        Band() => ""
        Layer() => ""
        Mesh() => ""
        Flow() => ""
        Pane() => ""
        Gap() => ""
        Label() => ""
        Glyph() => ""
        Draw() => ""

fn prism_node_role(n: PrismNode) -> string
    prism_node_role_of(n.kind)

// PRISM_SPEC.md §16 resource bounds. Named, not sprinkled as magic numbers, so a later
// milestone can make them a configurable capability (spec §16: "configurable with a safe
// default") without hunting for every comparison site.
let PRISM_MAX_TREE_DEPTH = 256
let PRISM_MAX_NODE_COUNT = 100000

// The node-tree value. See the header for why this is a struct (not an all-enum ADT), why
// `children` is a bare `list`, and why `depth`/`count` are cached rather than recomputed.
type PrismNode
    kind: PrismNodeKind
    attrs: dict
    children: list
    key: string?
    depth: int
    count: int

// ── Constructors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The one real constructor -- every constructor below calls this. Computes this node's
// subtree depth/count from its (already bounds-checked) children, then enforces
// PRISM_MAX_TREE_DEPTH / PRISM_MAX_NODE_COUNT before the node is allowed to exist at all.
// A hostile or accidentally-huge tree fails here with a typed error, never a crash and never
// an unbounded allocation (PRISM_SPEC.md §16).
//
// ── WHY THE FIRST LINE OF THE LOOP IS A TYPE GUARD, NOT A FIELD READ ──────────────────────
// `children: list` is deliberately untyped (see header: no verified precedent for
// `list<PrismNode>`), which means EVERY widget constructor across all 22 primitives -- and
// therefore any caller of any of them -- can hand this function a list containing something
// that is not a PrismNode at all (an int, a string, a bare dict, ...). Before this guard
// existed, the loop below read `c.depth` first: on a non-PrismNode element that is a read
// through a struct-shaped field offset into memory that was never laid out as a PrismNode --
// a wild pointer dereference, observed directly as `prism_arrange.prism_stack([1, 2, 3])`
// segfaulting with ACCESS VIOLATION (0xC0000005) rather than returning `Err`. This is exactly
// the crash PRISM_SPEC.md §16 forbids ("a typed error... never a crash"), and it is the one
// chokepoint every constructor funnels through, so the guard here closes the hole for all 22
// primitives at once rather than needing 22 duplicate checks upstream. Verified fix: `type_name`
// reliably reports "PrismNode" for a real node and a distinct primitive name ("int", "string",
// "list", "dict", "float") for every hostile value tried -- a single string compare per child,
// still O(children), no second pass over the list.
fn prism_node_new(kind: PrismNodeKind, attrs: dict, children: list, key: string?) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let max_child_depth = 0
    let total_count = 1
    for idx, c in children
        if type_name(c) != "PrismNode"
            return err("prism_node: child " + str(idx) + " is not a PrismNode (got " + type_name(c) + ")")
        if c.depth > max_child_depth
            max_child_depth = c.depth
        total_count += c.count
    let depth = max_child_depth + 1
    if depth > PRISM_MAX_TREE_DEPTH
        return err("prism_node: tree depth " + str(depth) + " exceeds max " + str(PRISM_MAX_TREE_DEPTH))
    if total_count > PRISM_MAX_NODE_COUNT
        return err("prism_node: node count " + str(total_count) + " exceeds max " + str(PRISM_MAX_NODE_COUNT))
    ok(PrismNode(kind, attrs, children, key, depth, total_count))

// A childless node (`label`, `art`, `glyph`, an empty `gap`, ...), no identity key.
fn prism_node_leaf(kind: PrismNodeKind, attrs: dict) -> Result<PrismNode>
    prism_node_new(kind, attrs, [], none())

// A node with children and no identity key -- most container widgets (`stack`, `band`, ...).
fn prism_node_branch(kind: PrismNodeKind, attrs: dict, children: list) -> Result<PrismNode>
    prism_node_new(kind, attrs, children, none())

// A node with a runtime identity key -- what `each`/`grid` rows attach (PRISM_SPEC.md §12.6,
// §10: "`by` is mandatory on `each` and `grid`. A missing identity selector is a COMPILE
// ERROR" -- MA.3's widget layer enforces the compile-time obligation; this constructor is
// simply where the resulting runtime key value gets threaded onto the node).
fn prism_node_keyed(kind: PrismNodeKind, attrs: dict, children: list, key: string) -> Result<PrismNode>
    prism_node_new(kind, attrs, children, some(key))

// ── Accessors ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_node_kind(n: PrismNode) -> PrismNodeKind
    n.kind

fn prism_node_kind_str(n: PrismNode) -> string
    prism_node_kind_name(n.kind)

fn prism_node_children(n: PrismNode) -> list
    n.children

fn prism_node_child_count(n: PrismNode) -> int
    len(n.children)

fn prism_node_key(n: PrismNode) -> string?
    n.key

fn prism_node_has_key(n: PrismNode) -> bool
    match n.key
        Some(_) => true
        None => false

fn prism_node_depth(n: PrismNode) -> int
    n.depth

fn prism_node_count(n: PrismNode) -> int
    n.count

// Pre-order walk: invoke `visit` on this node, then recurse into each child in source order.
// A convenience for backends that render top-down (the HTML renderer at render/, the ANSI
// backend) so they don't each hand-roll the same recursion over `children`.
fn prism_node_walk(n: PrismNode, visit)
    visit(n)
    for c in n.children
        prism_node_walk(c, visit)

// ── Attribute accessors (typed where possible) ────────────────────────────────────────────
// `attrs` stays a generic dict at THIS layer by design (see header, design decision #1) --
// one node type serves 22 differently-shaped widgets. These typed getters are the boundary:
// MA.3's widget constructors read through them with an explicit default rather than every
// caller re-deriving its own "is this the right type, and is the key even present" check
// inline, matching the low-level-dict-getter convention std/core and forge_html already use
// (forge_html's `_attrs`/`_attr_pair` treat an attrs dict the same way).

fn prism_node_has_attr(n: PrismNode, name: string) -> bool
    name in n.attrs

fn prism_node_attr_str(n: PrismNode, name: string, default_val: string) -> string
    if name in n.attrs
        let v = n.attrs[name]
        if is_string(v)
            return v
    default_val

fn prism_node_attr_int(n: PrismNode, name: string, default_val: int) -> int
    if name in n.attrs
        let v = n.attrs[name]
        if is_int(v)
            return v
    default_val

// A NOVA bool read INLINE (e.g. straight out of a dict index, as here) reports `type_name`
// "int", not "bool" -- it only shows as "bool" once passed across a function-call boundary.
// (Verified directly: `is_bool()` on a dict-literal `true` read back via `attrs["k"]` returned
// false in this file's own KAT until this was accounted for.) So this accepts EITHER
// representation, same fix already used for exactly this duality in forge_orm's PG bool
// param encoding -- an int is only treated as the bool encoding when it is literally 0 or 1,
// so a genuine int-valued attribute (a `range` bound of 0, say) is never misread as a bool.
fn prism_node_attr_bool(n: PrismNode, name: string, default_val: bool) -> bool
    if name in n.attrs
        let v = n.attrs[name]
        let tn = type_name(v)
        if tn == "bool"
            return v
        if tn == "int" and (v == 0 or v == 1)
            return v == 1
    default_val
